  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nBy late monday night/early Tuesday morning another upper level \ntrough will push inland with models now showing the core of this \nnext system lifting to the NE through NV and UT. However the bottom \nof the trough will move through Arizona providing enough ascent to \nthe region to increase PoPs back to between 60-90% across South-\nCentral AZ, and 20-60% across SW AZ. PWATs Tuesday will also \nincrease to over 200%, with the latest WPC QPF indicating Tuesday's \nrain totals between 0.25\"-0.50\" across the Greater Phoenix area, \nlocally higher in thunderstorms, and close to 1.00\" over Central AZ \nhigher terrain to the north/northeast. However, considerable spread \nremains in the model guidance, with 25th-75th percentile QPF ranging \nbetween a few tenths to upwards of 1.25\", focused over the higher \nterrain to the north/northeast of Phoenix. A 1-2 day break in the \nunsettled weather will then likely ensue following Tuesday, ahead of \nthe next system. \n\nDuring the latter half of the week, ensembles are pointing towards \nyet another upper level trough moving inland from the Pacific NW, \nhowever, there are still considerable discrepancies in the \ntrajectory of this followup system (how far south it will propagate, \nwhich will impact the moisture return and placement of the best \nupper level ascent mechanisms). Due to this, confidence remains low \nfor PoPs/QPF late in the work week, but at the very least, there is \nbuilding consensus for an even cooler airmass to enter the region as \na result of this second system. Latest NBM highs continue to trend \ntowards the 60s to lower 70s by late week. However, the spread in \nthe model guidance increases considerably on Friday, with IQRs on \nthe NBM forecast temperatures increasing to 8F-10F.",
